  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Number of gender-sensitive Water Strategy developed and supporting a decentralized approach to water governance and that is gender-sensitive
2.  Number of Institutions capacitated to implement a gender sensitive National Water Strategy, integrated water resource management and decentralized water governance 
3. Number of awareness workshops/meetings and events held 
4. Number and type of physical livelihood assets constructed to reduce the impacts of floods and droughts
5. Number of community resource persons trained on issues around Climate Change adaptation, Land management and exploitation of the mild and hide value chains (disaggregated by gender</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2019">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. There is no IWRM strategy or plan in any state
2. Capacity gaps result in the lack of water governance frameworks and fragmented water resources management, planning and coordination at federal and states levels
3. Limited awareness about impacts of climate change on water resources and Integrated Water Resource Management principles  
4. There is little functioning and climate-proofed physical infrastructure available in the target regions to mobilize surface water and groundwater and to divert flood waters for agro-pastoralists.
5. The agro-pastoral communities have no capacity to develop diversified pastoral practices, taking advantage of the value chain (e.g., milk, meat, cheeses, hides). There are no specialized trainers on the milk and hide value chains to maintain and transfer knowledge.</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. A framework for a gender-sensitive National IWRM Strategy is developed and an update is made to one of the Water Resources Policies for either Somaliland or Puntland.
2. 7 water mandated Ministries (Ministry of Energy Water resource-federal government of Somalia, and water ministries in Galmudug, Hirshabelle, South West, Jubaland, Puntland, and Somaliland) 
3. 3workshops/meetings held for policy makers and 6 events held with the communities for awareness raising on the issues of climate change and water management

4. 1 new borehole and 1 rehabilitated borehole,
2 earth dams and 2 rehabilitated earth dams,
3 new berkeds, and 1 canal rehabilitation
5. Three ToTs trained on agro-pastoral value chain exploitation nominated in each village (22 training recipients initially, at least 30% women)</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. A validated gender-sensitive National Water Strategy has been developed and available with the federal ministry of Energy and water Resources. The strategy incorporates the priorities of federal member states 
2. Through provision of operational and technical support by the project, 9 line ministries and Institutions were capacitated and are now implementing full-scale activities. 
3. Two coordination meetings and three workshops were held were held . More than 240 (112 women) of water resources-related stakeholders, such as Federal and FMS Ministries, media, the private sector, civil societies, IGOs, Academia , Training Institutes, and UN Agencies attended. These coordination meetings and workshops have contributed to the creation of the consensus conductive to the elaboration and validation of the gender sensitive National Water Strategy. Furthermore, ley achieved results through these events includes: 
Somalia Water Resources Compact committed by all engaged stakeholders and government counterparts in the wate sector to support water resources development in Somalia; 2) National Dialogue that incorporates a country wide exchange of ideas, information, and experiences, in turn generating improved capacity, empowerment and cooperation; 3) National Water Resources Strategic Plan embedding Water Governance; Water Services; Resilience and Climate Change, and IWRM; 4)Water Resources Development Road Map aligned to the IWRM strategy
4. Somaliland: Water harvesting infrastructures were constructed or rehabilitated helping approximately 3,000 households (1470 women headed households). These included: 
- 1 earth dam rehabilitation with auxiliaries including, -silt trap, solar system, and water kiosk
- I water diversion
5. 306 community resource persons (40% women) benefited an Integrated Water Resources rangeland management, and Livestock Value chain trainings. The trainings introduced coordinated approach to land and water resources management at national and community level and serves as an equitable access and sustainable use vs water demand and conflicts over the scarce water resources as well as improving diversification of livelihoods.
These included: 
-132 persons at Cel-bilcile and Beer in Somaliland
-174 persons at Daroor and Dhahar in Puntland 
6. rehabilitation of rangelands of up to 30 ha was completed in Puntland. The rangeland rehabilitations of gully erosion control structures have created short-term employment through cash for work to support the livelihoods of the Covid 19 affected community. Around 950 workers (40% women) in Puntland benefited the Cash for work schemes
-270 farmers (40% women) benefited livelihood enhancement and sensitisation to expand their agricultural productivity by emphasizing the importance of the staple food production during the Covid 19
- tillage support was provided to 10 vulnerable farmers (women) who were affected by the COVID 19 pandemics in six sites in Nugal, Sool, Sanag and Bari. A total of 30 ha of land were covered and prepared for cultivation. 
7.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. A multi-sectorial IWRM strategy, linked to the National Water Policy endorsed and published
2. Three (3) coordination workshops building IWRM capacities at the national and regional levels (at least 30% women participation) 
3. Target policy makers and planners at the national and district levels have knowledge on how to manage water in the context of climate change (at least 30% women participation)</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Achieved  (A gender-sensitive National IWRM Strategy or National Water Resources Strategy has been developed , translated , published and owned by the Federal Ministry of Energy and Water Resources)
2. Achieved  ((3) coordination workshops were held engaging 334 stakeholders (42% women participants).
3. surpassed by (1.25% equivalent to 94 policy markers (30 women participation) have improved understanding in managing water in the context of climate change)</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Strategy is developed supporting a decentralized approach to water governance
2. Densification of water resources monitoring by 50%. Procurement and installation of 6 AWS, 5 manual rain gauges, 4 synoptic stations, 2 radar river level sensors and 3 groundwater sensors to improve groundwater and surface water data collection
3. improved water management and livelihood diversification for agro-pastoralists</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Achieved: A gender-sensitive National Water Resources Strategy (NWRS, 2021-25) has been developed and approved by the Federal Government of Somalia setting the priorities for water governance, integrated water resources management and water services with a 5- and 10-year Road Map
2.	Under process: In collaboration with the MoEWR-FGS, UNDP Somalia CO has initiated the procurement process which is planned to conclude at end of December 2022.
3. The project Midterm target has been exceeded. Across Somalia, 13 water harvesting infrastructures and nature-based solutions were optimized serving more than 170,500 households (50% women-headed households representing (Somaliland: 91,598HH; Puntland:78,902HH)</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
